# HealOps Audit Dashboard

Full skill: `.claude/skills/healops-nextjs-dashboard/SKILL.md`

The dashboard's job is to make a claim checkable: *the system fixed three of five incidents
automatically, and here is the evidence for each one.* Every design decision serves that.

Node 24 LTS, Next.js 16.3.x App Router (no Pages Router), TypeScript strict, Tailwind + shadcn/ui.
**shadcn/ui components are copied into `components/ui` and edited there, never an npm dependency.**

## Layout

```
dashboard/src/
├── app/
│   ├── layout.tsx                    # dark theme root, nav
│   ├── page.tsx                      # main incident timeline
│   ├── incidents/[id]/page.tsx       # incident detail
│   ├── runbooks/page.tsx             # catalog + stats + promotion state
│   ├── approvals/page.tsx            # pending approvals
│   └── api/                          # incidents  executions  stats  health
├── components/
│   ├── timeline/                     # IncidentTimeline  TimelineEvent  ExecutionDetail
│   ├── dashboard/                    # KPICards  RunbookSuccessChart
│   │                                 # IncidentFrequency  ApprovalPending
│   ├── common/                       # StatusBadge  JsonDiff  LoadingSkeleton
│   └── ui/                           # shadcn/ui, COPIED IN and edited here
├── hooks/                            # useIncidents  useExecutions  useStats
└── types/index.ts
```

## Server-first, and where that breaks

Default to Server Components. Reach for `'use client'` only for genuine interactivity: the live
incident feed, `JsonDiff` expand/collapse state, timeline filters, approval buttons, anything holding
`useState`.

Route Handlers under `app/api/` **proxy the engine** rather than talking to PostgreSQL directly. That
keeps one query layer, one set of types, and means the public deployment can point at a read-only
seeded API with no cluster credentials anywhere in the front end.

Add `generateMetadata` on the incident detail route so a shared link previews as the incident it is.

## The incident timeline is the centrepiece

Render the pipeline as a **horizontal state trail**, because that trail *is* the differentiator made
visible:

```
correlate -> plan -> policy -> [shadow|approval] -> execute -> verify -> {remediated|reverted|escalated}
```

Design for the stages that do nothing:

- **Refused** — the runbook declined, with the reason. A first-class outcome, not an error state.
- **Denied** — policy blocked it, with the rule id and message that fired.
- **Shadow** — "would have done X", visually distinct from anything that acted.
- **Reverted** — verification failed and compensation ran. Make it **legible rather than alarming**:
  it is the system catching its own mistake.

Symptom alerts nest under the cause with the correlation reason, never as separate rows — the whole
point of correlation is that forty alerts are one incident.

## KPI cards

Mirror the efficacy scorecard with honest denominators visible on hover or in a caption. Auto-resolution
rate excludes human-approved fixes. **False-remediation rate is a single big number, green at zero** —
the trust metric. MTTR auto vs escalated side by side.

**Do not style escalation or refusal as failures.** A red "escalation rate" card would contradict the
project's entire thesis.

## Components worth getting right

- **`JsonDiff`** renders `state_before` vs `state_after`. Highlight only changed paths, collapse
  unchanged subtrees by default, make changed lines copyable. This is how a reviewer confirms a
  compensation restored state exactly.
- **`StatusBadge`** covers the full vocabulary — `REMEDIATED`, `NOT_RECOVERED`, `INCONCLUSIVE`,
  `REVERTED`, `REVERT_FAILED`, `ESCALATED`, `REFUSED`, `DENIED`, `SHADOW`, `PENDING`. Drive it from a
  **discriminated union** so a new outcome is a compile error, not a silently grey badge.
- **`ApprovalPending`** shows the plan summary, blast radius, cost delta, expected recovery signal, and
  the countdown to `expires_at`. **Read the deadline from the API** — PostgreSQL is the source of
  truth, so never compute expiry client-side from a duration.
- **Runbook catalog** lists triggers, reversibility class, chaos fixture, and promotion mode per
  namespace. This is the model card rendered as UI.

## Styling

Dark-first. Theme through **semantic tokens in CSS variables**, never hardcoded colours — the outcome
vocabulary needs consistent semantics across badges, timeline nodes and charts, and that only holds if
there is one source of colour truth. Preserve Radix accessibility primitives: keyboard navigation on
the timeline, focus management in the approval dialog, `aria-live` on the incident feed.

## States that actually happen

Build all four for every data view: **Loading** (skeleton matching the final layout), **Empty** ("no
incidents yet" is the *normal* state of a healthy cluster — make it say how to trigger the demo),
**Error** (degrade to cached data with a staleness note, never a blank page), **Stale** (show the age).

## Read-only in public, always

Approval buttons must be **absent or disabled** in the public build, not merely unwired. Gate write
affordances behind a build-time flag so the read-only build cannot render them at all.
